<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Afrasiyab dynasty of Basra</h1><p class="st-meta">1596 CE – 1668 CE (approx.) · capital Basra</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A family of hereditary pashas who made Basra a nearly independent port state under nominal Ottoman rule for seventy years.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1596 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrasiyab-of-basra">Afrasiyab</a>, a local official of uncertain origin, bought the governorship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a> from the Ottoman governor, who could no longer hold the remote province. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrasiab">Afrasiyab</a> and his descendants ruled as hereditary pashas, sending token tribute to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a> flourished as a free-trading port between the Gulf, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and the Ottoman lands. Ali Pasha (c. 1624 to 1650) welcomed Portuguese, English and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> merchants, patronised poets and scholars and resisted Safavid attacks. The pashas relied on Arab tribal allies and paid little attention to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>. Their rule saw Basra become a major centre for the trade in horses, dates and Indian textiles.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Husayn Pasha's harsh rule provoked revolts and Ottoman intervention. In 1668 an Ottoman expedition took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a>, and Husayn fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, ending the dynasty.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="principality, 1596 CE – 1668 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">principality</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesopotamia and Iran</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden=